# Grid resolver prototype — #vi-sysdiag-grid, step 1 of #vi-sysdiag-grid-mvp.
# Hand-authored, no tool assistance, to measure what authoring actually costs.
#
# Two sections. CONTENT is Mermaid flowchart syntax: what exists, what connects to
# what, what it says. GRID is placement: which cell holds what. The split is
# #penrose's Substance/Style, and neither half can express the other's job.

content
  flowchart TD
    subgraph browser [BROWSER]
      mv["map view | the decision tree, the 3D graph, and one card's full text"]
      cp["chat pane | where you type, and where the reply streams back"]
    end
    subgraph server [SERVER PROCESS]
      st["state | GET /api/graph, one fetch of today's folded map"]
      lv["live + sse | POST /api/chat and /api/ask, and the SSE stream back"]
    end
    mv -->|loads the folded map| st
    cp -->|sends what you type| lv
    lv -->|streams the reply back| cp

    class mv,cp read
    class st state
    class lv agent

# An edge's id is <from>-><to>. Mermaid does not name edges, and it does not need to:
# the pair is already unique and already written above. `->` (not `_`) is what
# #vi-sysdiag-grid-gaps's gap 5 asked for: no card id can contain `->`, so a grid token
# is unambiguous, always — a card id containing `_` used to collide with a derived edge id.
#
# Four tokens, everything else is derived:
#   .        empty cell
#   <card>   a cell of that card
#   <edge>   a cell of that edge's path
#   @<edge>  a cell of that edge's label
#
# Corners, junctions and arrowheads are inferred from neighbours, per #svgbob.
# Group frames are the bounding box of their members, per the subgraph above.

grid
  "mv mv      mv . . cp      cp cp      . ."
  "mv mv      mv . . cp      cp cp      . ."
  ".  mv->st  .  . . cp->lv  .  lv->cp  . ."
  ".  @mv->st .  . . @cp->lv .  @lv->cp . ."
  ".  mv->st  .  . . cp->lv  .  lv->cp  . ."
  "st st      st . . lv      lv lv      . ."
  "st st      st . . lv      lv lv      . ."
